 ## Overview
-This method modifies the covariance structure of 3D Gaussian primitives to enforce a half-ellipsoid geometry, aligning the Gaussian shape with local surface normals to better model sharp transitions between object surfaces and empty space [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard 3D Gaussian Splatting by replacing full ellipsoids with truncated shapes that eliminate "dust" and noise artifacts common in unstructured point cloud reconstructions [S1]. Unlike [Hard and Soft Depth Regularization](9178-hard-and-soft-depth-regularization.md) which constrains positions, this method directly regularizes the shape parameters [S1].
+This method regularizes the covariance matrices of 3D Gaussians to enforce a "half-Gaussian" shape, effectively modeling sharp transitions between object surfaces and empty space by cutting ellipsoids with a plane aligned to the surface normal [S1]. It addresses the limitation of full ellipsoids in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting, which often produce blurry boundaries or "ghosting" artifacts at object edges [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on frequency domain constraints or depth supervision, this approach modifies the geometric structure of the primitives themselves during training [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-*   $\Sigma$: The $3 \times 3$ covariance matrix of a Gaussian primitive in world space.
-*   $n$: The local surface normal vector at the Gaussian center.
+The method transforms the standard 3D Gaussian covariance matrix into a local coordinate system defined by the surface normal, modifies the diagonal elements to zero out components along the normal direction, and transforms it back. This process is applied periodically during training.
 
-**Outputs:**
-*   $\sigma$: The regularized covariance matrix in world space, representing a half-ellipsoid.
+**Inputs and Outputs**
+*   **Input:** The current 3D Gaussian covariance matrix $\Sigma$ (3x3 symmetric positive definite), and the surface normal vector $n$ at the Gaussian's center.
+*   **Output:** The regularized covariance matrix $\sigma$ (3x3), representing a half-Gaussian.
 
-**Data Structures:**
-*   **Basis Matrix ($B$):** A $3 \times 3$ matrix where columns are the orthonormal vectors $v_1, v_2, n$.
-*   **Covariance in Basis ($cov$):** A $3 \times 3$ matrix representing $\Sigma$ rotated into the basis defined by $B$.
-*   **Regularized Covariance in Basis ($\sigma_{basis}$):** A $3 \times 3$ matrix with specific zeroed entries to enforce the half-Gaussian constraint.
+**Data Structures**
+*   **Covariance Matrix:** A 3x3 symmetric matrix. In the local basis, it is stored as a 3x3 matrix where the third dimension corresponds to the normal direction.
+*   **Basis Matrix:** A 3x3 orthogonal matrix constructed from two tangent vectors and the normal vector.
 
-**Parameters:**
-*   **Iteration Interval:** The transformation is applied every 5000 iterations [S1].
-*   **Opacity Threshold:** Increased to 0.01 for densification and pruning [S1].
-*   **Half-Gaussian Opacity Reset:** Set to 0.02 for newly created or transformed half-Gaussians [S1].
